'''2†.''' Artificial, as opposed to ''natural;'' produced or producing by art: as, “too ''artful'' a writer,” ''Dryden'', Life of Virgil.—'''3.''' Skilful. (''a'') Of persons, skilful in adapting means to ends; adroit. (''b'') Of things, skilfully adapted; ingenious; clever. Hence—'''4.''' Cunning; crafty; practising or characterized by art or stratagem: as, “the ''Artful'' Dodger,” ''Dickens'', Oliver Twist.
{{smaller block|Fair to no purpose, ''artful'' to no end.
{{right|''Pope'', Moral Essays, iv. 116.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 4.''' ''Cunning'', ''Artful'', ''Sly'', etc. (see ''cunning''), deceitful, politic, shifty, insidious.}}

'''artfully''' (ärt′fu̇l-i), ''adv.'' In an artful manner. {{smaller|(''a'') With art or skill: as, colors ''artfully'' distributed on the canvas. [Rare.] (''b'') With cunning or craft; craftily; cunningly.}}
{{smaller block|Whether this motion was honestly made by the Opposition . . . or ''artfully'' made by the courtiers, . . . it is now impossible to discover. {{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., vi.}}}}

'''artfulness''' (ärt′fu̇l-nes), ''n.'' The quality of being artful; craft; cunning; address.

'''arthen†''' (är′then), ''a.'' An old form of ''earthen.''

'''arthra''', ''n.'' Plural of ''arthron.''

'''arthral''' (är′thral), ''a.'' [< ''arthron'' + ''-al.''] Of or pertaining to an arthron or articulation; articular: as, “the ''arthral'' surface of the ilium,”
